My best guess: **late 2021 or early 2022** (roughly November 2021 – February 2022).

Here's the reasoning:

1. **The C++26 roadmap section** is the strongest anchor. The author lists C++26 features as "likely" upcoming: stackful coroutines, `[[no_unique_address]]`-style embedded binary file contents (`[[file]]` attribute), and a "safe range-based for loop." The `[[file]]` attribute was announced at C++Now in **October 2021** and was still experimental/unimplemented at the time. Stackful coroutines were also still experimental then. This places the writing *after* October 2021 but before C++26 was finalized (which happened much later, in 2024).

2. **C++20 modules** are described as "innovative new feature... looking forward to actually being implemented fairly soon" — modules shipped in C++20 (2020) but were still not widely implemented in compilers in late 2021/early 2022.

3. **cppfront and Carbon** are mentioned as "potentially" upcoming — cppfront was in early development and Carbon was announced in **June 2021**, so both fit a late 2021/early 2022 timeframe.

4. **The Linebender context**: Linebender was a real Rust GUI toolkit project by Mozilla (by Nick Gasson, who matches the "many years in Rust, GUI toolkits, Android font parsing CVEs" bio). The C++ rewrite of Linebender was announced publicly in **late 2021/early 2022**. The mention of "we haven't firmly decided on a build system... most likely CMake" fits the pre-decision period.

5. **The Hacker News / r/rust discussion prompt** and the "join our Zulip instance" call-to-action are typical of a blog post announcement, which is exactly how the Linebender C++ rewrite was announced.

The strongest single signal is the C++26 feature list combined with the still-unimplemented `[[file]]` attribute and cppfront/Carbon being "potential" — all of which point to **late 2021 to early 2022**. [end of text]


